1
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:10,160
We're going to enhance our customer service
agent by giving it an exchange capability.

2
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,760
Let's start with the policy.

3
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:15,075
So our store allows exchanges within 30
calendar

4
00:00:15,075 --> 00:00:18,320
days of delivery and excludes final sale
items.

5
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:23,840
We also need to ensure that the requested
quantity hasn't already been exchanged.

6
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:28,320
We'll enforce these rules using the data
that's stored in our database.

7
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,598
These guardrails make our exchange service
reject requests that violate the policy,

8
00:00:33,598 --> 00:00:35,760
even if the model asks to proceed.

9
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,562
First, we retrieve the order the customer is
asking

10
00:00:38,562 --> 00:00:41,200
about and verify that it's actually their
order.

11
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,947
Our service receives the customer identity
from the session,

12
00:00:44,947 --> 00:00:48,320
so the model doesn't choose whose order it
can access.

13
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,548
And remember, get order uses both the
customer ID and the order ID in

14
00:00:52,548 --> 00:00:56,960
its database query to return only an order
that belongs to the customer.

15
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,640
These checks enforce the policy against the
purchase item.

16
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:05,480
Even if the customer persuades the model to
exchange a final sale item,

17
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,480
the service is going to reject that request.

18
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:12,269
The same checks apply regardless of how the
customer

19
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:16,640
phrases the request or which tools the model
called earlier.

20
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:21,397
Then the check eligibility function returns
whether the request passed

21
00:01:21,397 --> 00:01:25,135
those checks and if it didn't it provides
the reason so

22
00:01:25,135 --> 00:01:28,465
that gives the agent a result that it can
explain

23
00:01:28,465 --> 00:01:33,426
to the customer right passing these checks
doesn't authorize an exchange,

24
00:01:33,426 --> 00:01:35,600
it only establishes eligibility.

25
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:39,957
The eligibility rules are fixed, so we
enforce those in code,

26
00:01:39,957 --> 00:01:43,600
but choosing a replacement involves a
conversation.

27
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:48,220
For example, the customer might want a
larger size, another color,

28
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:51,440
or maybe some help with exploring the
options.

29
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,565
So we need our agent to be able to interpret

30
00:01:54,565 --> 00:01:59,040
that request and query our inventory with
the relevant filters.

31
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:02,102
We've registered the inventory tool on the
agent,

32
00:02:02,102 --> 00:02:07,040
which gives the model access to the tool so
it can look up replacement options.

33
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:14,000
Here, a size 9 has 3 pairs available while
size 10 is out of stock.

34
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,880
Both sizes exist in the catalog but only
size 9 is available for an exchange.

35
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:22,035
The inventory tool gives our agent that
information

36
00:02:22,035 --> 00:02:24,880
so that it can offer an available
replacement.

37
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:27,552
The tool accepts size and color filters,

38
00:02:27,552 --> 00:02:32,160
which keeps the model's request close to
what the customer described.

39
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:35,386
It also returns the product IDs from the
database,

40
00:02:35,386 --> 00:02:40,160
which gives the agent the exact values to
use when requesting an exchange.

41
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,320
We don't want it trying to make 1 up, right?

42
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,268
As for the quote for the exchange,

43
00:02:44,268 --> 00:02:47,705
we calculate it using the amount paid for
the original item,

44
00:02:47,705 --> 00:02:51,600
the current replacement price, and then the
configured exchange fee.

45
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:56,960
And this is handled in code because, well,
frankly, it can be, right?

46
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:00,320
Basically anything that you can do in code,
you should.

47
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:05,520
This is a deterministic calculation that
does not change given the same input,

48
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:08,720
so there's no need for the model to handle
this.

49
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:11,636
The agent's job is to explain the return

50
00:03:11,636 --> 00:03:15,280
quote using the amounts that the service
provides.

51
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:17,280
And the quote is just a proposal.

52
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:20,067
The customer still needs to accept those
exact

53
00:03:20,067 --> 00:03:23,520
details before the service changes anything
in the store.

54
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:28,080
Now consider the customer's reason for
requesting an exchange.

55
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:31,070
These shoes squeezed my toes is different
from the

56
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:34,000
soles separated on the first day of wearing
them.

57
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,111
Both customers might be requesting another
size,

58
00:03:37,111 --> 00:03:40,870
but the second customer has reported a
product defect that

59
00:03:40,870 --> 00:03:44,240
our policy says must be sent to a person for
review.

60
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:46,435
To choose the right handling path,

61
00:03:46,435 --> 00:03:51,342
we need to classify the customer's
description as a fit preference issue,

62
00:03:51,342 --> 00:03:55,280
a product defect, or just something that
needs clarification.

63
00:03:55,280 --> 00:04:01,127
Our LLM could classify the issue, but since
we have a fixed set of options here,

64
00:04:01,127 --> 00:04:05,440
it's actually a good task to consider a
decision model for.

65
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:08,752
A decision model evaluates information
against

66
00:04:08,752 --> 00:04:11,920
criteria and it returns a structured answer.

67
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:13,575
For our exchange agent,

68
00:04:13,575 --> 00:04:20,051
we'll use the Jev decision model to identify
the kind of issue that the customer
reported,

69
00:04:20,051 --> 00:04:24,800
which then gives our code info it can use to
choose the next step.

70
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:29,394
We have a function to classify issue which
calls Jev through System 1,

71
00:04:29,394 --> 00:04:33,200
which is the type of decision model that
Jev's belongs to.

72
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:37,985
State contains the customer's message, which
is what we want Jev to evaluate,

73
00:04:37,985 --> 00:04:40,720
and choice is for multiple choice questions.

74
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:43,792
We use it here because we want Jev to pick 1

75
00:04:43,792 --> 00:04:47,615
of our issue categories and we give the
question a name,

76
00:04:47,615 --> 00:04:50,960
issue, so we can find its answer in the
response.

77
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:55,040
Jev also supports other types of questions
beyond multiple choice.

78
00:04:55,040 --> 00:05:00,846
You can use a score question to have Jev
rate something along levels that we define,

79
00:05:00,846 --> 00:05:03,680
such as how frustrated a customer sounds.

80
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:08,467
And Bool answers a yes or no question such
as whether the customer is

81
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:13,600
asking for a refund and it returns the
probability that the answer is yes.

82
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:18,400
Let's see how those answers change with the
customer's message.

83
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:22,275
State contains the customer's message and
questions contains

84
00:05:22,275 --> 00:05:25,440
the question and the descriptions of the
choices.

85
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:28,080
These are the same descriptions we use in
our code.

86
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:32,320
So let's start with the customer whose shoes
squeeze their toes.

87
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:35,808
We're going to run the request and we see
that Jev

88
00:05:35,808 --> 00:05:40,800
selected fit preference even though the
customer never used the word fit.

89
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:45,440
It connected squeeze my toes with the
description of a fit issue.

90
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:50,800
That lets our agent handle different ways
that customers describe the same problem.

91
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:55,440
These percentages show how likely Jev thinks
each option is.

92
00:05:55,440 --> 00:06:01,760
Here the probability of fit preference being
correct is 100% while the others are 0%.

93
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:05,143
The probabilities of all of the options add
up

94
00:06:05,143 --> 00:06:09,040
to 100% and they can be split across several
options.

95
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:12,741
Jev selects the option with the highest
probability as

96
00:06:12,741 --> 00:06:16,240
its answers and it gives you a confidence
indicator.

97
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:21,040
So confidence summarizes how strongly the
probabilities favor 1 option.

98
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:25,760
It's high here because all of the
probability is on fit preference.

99
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:28,515
So that describes the model's prediction but

100
00:06:28,515 --> 00:06:31,520
it doesn't guarantee that the answer is
correct.

101
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:32,960
Let's try another 1.

102
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:36,684
Now for this 1 Jev selected a product
problem which

103
00:06:36,684 --> 00:06:41,840
is the correct answer because the customer
described a specific failure.

104
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:46,149
Based on our store's policy, if an item is
damaged,

105
00:06:46,149 --> 00:06:50,880
then we send the exchange request to a human
for review.

106
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:56,240
Using this answer from Jev, we can execute a
different branch of our code.

107
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,000
Let's try a vague 1.

108
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,440
Here the customer only says something is
wrong with their shoes.

109
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:07,760
Jev selected unclear, which is an option
that we provided under our questions.

110
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:10,409
Giving it that option lets the model return
a

111
00:07:10,409 --> 00:07:14,000
useful answer when the message doesn't
contain enough detail.

112
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:19,583
If we only offered fit and product problem,
then we'd be asking it to pick between

113
00:07:19,583 --> 00:07:23,600
explanations that the customer hasn't
necessarily given us.

114
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:28,200
So given this, we could have our agent ask,
what's wrong with the shoes,

115
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:32,800
to gather more information so that the model
can make a better decision.

116
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:36,063
Also notice here this is the first time that
we've

117
00:07:36,063 --> 00:07:40,240
seen the answer that wasn't 100% probability
or 100% confidence.

118
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:45,060
So you can see how the probabilities are
distributed across the options,

119
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:46,800
but they add up to be 100.

120
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,080
Okay, let's try 1 that's a bit ambiguous.

121
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:55,040
Okay, for this 1, the customer mentions both
tight shoes and a separated sole.

122
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:00,560
So Jev selected product problem, even though
the customer also described the fit issue.

123
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:05,467
If we look at our questions, the fit
description excludes a reported defect and
that

124
00:08:05,467 --> 00:08:09,440
distinction helps separate the choices when
a message mentions both.

125
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:13,600
So this shows that Jev actually follows that
distinction.

126
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:18,289
Each case checks a different behavior,
understanding everyday wording,

127
00:08:18,289 --> 00:08:22,174
recognizing a reported problem, asking for
missing detail,

128
00:08:22,174 --> 00:08:24,720
and handling overlapping descriptions.

129
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:27,747
When you use a decision model for your
agents,

130
00:08:27,747 --> 00:08:32,880
build your own test set around the
distinctions that your agent needs to make.

131
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:37,236
If an answer is wrong, check whether the
message has enough information and

132
00:08:37,236 --> 00:08:40,720
whether your choices describe the
distinction that you need.

133
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:44,080
And remember, a confident answer can still
be wrong.

134
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:46,627
So compare the choices with your expected

135
00:08:46,627 --> 00:08:49,920
answers before deciding how much confidence
is enough

136
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:52,720
that your agent should trust it.

137
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:57,760
The choice question type fits our exchange
question because we need a category.

138
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:02,800
But for something like deciding which
support requests need attention first,
right?

139
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,680
We might want a rating instead.

140
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:12,492
Here we're asking how frustrated the
customer sounds and we describe a calm
request,

141
00:09:12,492 --> 00:09:17,520
a frustrated but civil request, and then a
very angry request.

142
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:21,520
And these levels give the model something
